# Chat Math Output

This rule governs math in replies to the user. It does not govern source files whose language uses dollar math, such as Typst, which keeps `$...$` syntax.

In chat, use LaTeX delimiters the chat renderer supports:

- Inline: `\(...\)`
- Display: `\[...\]`

Do not use `$...$` or `$$...$$` in chat. Single-dollar math is shown as raw source.

Write LaTeX, not Typst, inside those delimiters: `\mathbf{z}`, `\mathcal{L}`, `\frac{a}{b}`. Do not wrap math in backticks.

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD — dollar delimiters render as source -->
下一词元是 $z_i^{t+\Delta t}$。

$$
\mathcal{L} = -\sum_i \log q_i
$$

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
下一词元是 \(z_i^{t+\Delta t}\)。

\[
\mathcal{L} = -\sum_i \log q_i
\]
```

Put each formula in its own delimiter pair. Keep a display formula on its own lines, with a blank line before and after the block.

Inside `\[...\]`, never put `=` or `-` alone on its own line. The chat Markdown parser treats `expr` followed by a standalone `=` or `-` line as a Setext heading (`<h1>` / `<h2>`), which prematurely closes `\[...\]` and renders the rest of the formula as headings and raw text. Write single-line equations on one line, or use `\begin{aligned}` with `&=`:

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD — standalone = and - trigger Setext headings and break \[...\] -->
\[
\mathbf{0}
=
\Bigl(\lim_{k\to\infty}\bar{\nu}_{N_k}\Bigr)Q
-
\lim_{k\to\infty}\bar{\nu}_{N_k}
\]

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
\[
\mathbf{0}
= \Bigl(\lim_{k\to\infty}\bar{\nu}_{N_k}\Bigr)Q - \lim_{k\to\infty}\bar{\nu}_{N_k}
\]
```
